yword. The replacement keeps the rhetorical question and the keyword in backticks.","alternative":"¿Se nos ha olvidado para qué sirve `export` en realidad?","confidence":0.8,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source sentence asks about the underlying purpose of exporting."}]}, {"id":"local-3","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Neat feature. Not the point.","targetQuote":"Función interesante. No es el punto.","replacement":"Función interesante. No viene al caso.","rationale":"'No es el punto' is a calque of 'not the point'. 'No viene al caso' is the idiomatic Spanish and keeps the curt rhythm.","alternative":"Muy bonito. Pero no es de lo que hablamos.","confidence":0.8,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source is a short dismissive aside."}]}, {"id":"local-4","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"> We are sending a signal to `import`ers _how to use a thing._","targetQuote":"> Le estamos enviando una señal a los `import`ers _sobre cómo usar algo._","replacement":"> Le estamos enviando una señal a quien hace `import` _sobre cómo usar algo._","rationale":"'`import`ers' carries an English suffix into Spanish prose and reads like a leftover. A relative clause keeps the keyword in backticks and reads naturally. The italics are kept.","alternative":"> Les estamos diciendo a quienes importan el módulo _cómo usar algo._","confidence":0.75,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source's 'importers' just means the consumers of the module."}]}, {"id":"local-5","kind":"translation","category":"faithfulness","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"A `named export` says it’s “definitely **_A THING!_**”","targetQuote":"Una `exportación nombrada` dice que es \"definitivamente **_UNA COSA_**.\"","replacement":"Un `named export` dice que es \"definitivamente **_¡UNA COSA!_**\".","rationale":"Two problems. First, the translation put a Spanish phrase inside code formatting. Either keep the English term in backticks, as the parallel `export default` bullet does, or drop the backticks. Second, the exclamation mark in 'A THING!' was lost, which flattens the joke. The period also belongs outside the closing quote in Spanish.","alternative":"Una exportación nombrada (`named export`) dice que es \"definitivamente **_¡UNA COSA!_**\".","confidence":0.7,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"Spanish technical prose","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The source has the term in backticks and 'A THING!' with an exclamation mark."},{"referenceId":"mdn-export-es","explanation":"Spanish MDN distinguishes named and default exports. Both 'exportación nombrada' and the English term are understood."}]}, {"id":"local-6","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Also raises some questions, “got any other buddies there?“ Follow up, “Are they invited or required?”","targetQuote":"También plantea algunas preguntas, \"¿tienes otros compañeros por ahí?\" Seguimiento: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","replacement":"También plantea algunas preguntas: \"¿Tienes más compañeros por ahí?\". Y la siguiente: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","rationale":"'Seguimiento:' is a noun-for-noun rendering of 'Follow up'. It sounds like a meeting agenda, not a follow-up question. The replacement also fixes the colon and the capital letter after the opening question mark.","alternative":"…Y, de paso: \"¿Están invitados o son obligatorios?\"","confidence":0.78,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"In the source, 'Follow up' introduces a second, related question."}]}, {"id":"local-7","kind":"translation","category":"faithfulness","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Now tell me, how do I hold your contraption again? No author intent.","targetQuote":"Ahora dime, ¿cómo sostengo tu invento otra vez? Sin intención del autor.","replacement":"A ver, dime otra vez: ¿por dónde se agarra tu invento? No transmite ninguna intención del autor.","rationale":"'Sin intención del autor' can be misread as 'done unintentionally by the author'. The point is that the pattern tells the reader nothing about what the author intended. 'Por dónde se agarra' keeps the knife-handle joke better than 'cómo sostengo'.","alternative":"Ahora dime, ¿cómo se sujeta tu invento? No comunica la intención del autor.","confidence":0.7,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The surrounding thesis is that exports signal the author's intent."}]}, {"id":"local-8","kind":"translation","category":"readability","severity":2,"sourceQuote":"Incorrect (any more). Configure/update your tools.","targetQuote":"Incorrecto (ya no más). Configura/actualiza tus herramientas.","replacement":"Ya no es cierto. Configura o actualiza tus herramientas.","rationale":"'Incorrecto (ya no más)' reads backwards: it seems to say it is no longer incorrect. The source means 'no longer true'.","alternative":"Incorrecto (a día de hoy). Configura o actualiza tus herramientas.","confidence":0.75,"requiresExternalEvidence":false,"audienceScope":"General Spanish","evidence":[{"referenceId":"source","explanation":"The next bullet says support has existed for 3+ years, which confirms the meaning is 'no longer true'."}]}, {"id":"local-9","kind":"translation","category":"idiomMetaphor","severity":1,"sourceQuote":"“Presenting ONE function w/ Single Purpose!”","targetQuote":"\"¡Presentando UNA función con un solo propósito!\"","replacement":"\"¡Les presento UNA función con un único propósito!\"","rationale":"A bare gerund as an announcement ('Presentando…') is an anglicism. A presenter's voice 